  await db.insert(lessonsTable).values([
    { courseId: cCourse.id, title: "Introduction to C", order: 1, language: "c", xpReward: 20, codeExample: `#include <stdio.h>\n\n// C is compiled, fast, and foundational\nint main() {\n    // Print output\n    printf("Hello, C World!\\n");\n    \n    // Variables with explicit types\n    int age = 25;\n    float price = 9.99f;\n    double pi = 3.14159265;\n    char grade = 'A';\n    char name[] = "Alice"; // string = char array\n    \n    // printf format specifiers\n    printf("Name: %s\\n", name);\n    printf("Age: %d\\n", age);\n    printf("Price: %.2f\\n", price);\n    printf("Pi: %lf\\n", pi);\n    printf("Grade: %c\\n", grade);\n    \n    return 0; // 0 means success\n}`, content: `C is one of the oldest and most influential programming languages. Created in 1972, C is still widely used for systems programming, embedded systems, and anywhere performance matters.\n\nWhy learn C?\n• Understand how computers really work\n• Foundation for C++, Java, Python, and more\n• Used in operating systems (Linux, Windows kernel)\n• Embedded systems (Arduino, microcontrollers)\n• Game engines, databases, compilers\n• Highest performance when needed\n\nC characteristics:\n• Compiled — you compile to machine code\n• Statically typed — types declared explicitly\n• Manual memory management — you control malloc/free\n• Low-level — can interact directly with hardware\n• No garbage collection — you manage memory\n• Fast — close to hardware performance\n\nEvery C program:\n• Starts with #include directives (like imports)\n• Has a main() function as entry point\n• Returns 0 from main for success\n• Uses printf() for output (not print or console.log)\n• Statements end with semicolons\n\nprintf format specifiers:\n• %d — integer\n• %f — float\n• %lf — double\n• %c — char\n• %s — string\n• %p — pointer address\n• %x — hexadecimal` },
    { courseId: cCourse.id, title: "Variables and Data Types", order: 2, language: "c", xpReward: 20, codeExample: `#include <stdio.h>\n#include <limits.h>  // for INT_MAX etc.\n\nint main() {\n    // Integer types\n    int a = 42;\n    short s = 100;\n    long l = 1000000L;\n    long long ll = 9000000000LL;\n    unsigned int u = 4294967295U;\n    \n    // Floating point\n    float f = 3.14f;     // 32-bit, ~7 decimal digits\n    double d = 3.14159;  // 64-bit, ~15 decimal digits\n    \n    // Character\n    char c = 'X';\n    printf("Char: %c, ASCII: %d\\n", c, c); // X, 88\n    \n    // Integer sizes\n    printf("int: %d bytes\\n", sizeof(int));\n    printf("long: %d bytes\\n", sizeof(long));\n    printf("double: %d bytes\\n", sizeof(double));\n    \n    // Integer limits\n    printf("INT_MAX = %d\\n", INT_MAX);\n    printf("INT_MIN = %d\\n", INT_MIN);\n    \n    // Constants\n    const double PI = 3.14159265358979;\n    printf("Pi = %lf\\n", PI);\n    \n    // Type casting\n    int x = 7, y = 2;\n    printf("%d / %d = %d\\n", x, y, x/y);         // 3 (integer!)\n    printf("%d / %d = %.2f\\n", x, y, (float)x/y); // 3.50\n    \n    return 0;\n}`, content: `C has a rich set of data types with explicit sizes. Understanding them is crucial for low-level programming.\n\nInteger types:\n• char — 1 byte (-128 to 127)\n• short — 2 bytes (-32,768 to 32,767)\n• int — 4 bytes (-2.1B to 2.1B)\n• long — 4 or 8 bytes (platform-dependent)\n• long long — 8 bytes (huge numbers)\n• Add unsigned for positive-only (doubles the range)\n\nFloating point:\n• float — 4 bytes, ~7 significant digits\n• double — 8 bytes, ~15 significant digits (preferred)\n• long double — 10 or 16 bytes (extended precision)\n\nCharacters:\n• Stored as integers (ASCII values)\n• 'A' = 65, 'a' = 97, '0' = 48\n• Single quotes for chars, double for strings\n\nsizeof operator:\n• Returns bytes of a type or variable\n• sizeof(int), sizeof(x) — no function call overhead\n\nType casting:\n• Implicit: int + double = double\n• Explicit: (double)x — cast x to double\n• Important: (float)a/b ≠ a/(float)b sometimes\n\nConstants:\n• const int MAX = 100; — type-safe\n• #define MAX 100 — preprocessor (no type)\n• Prefer const over #define in modern C` },
    { courseId: cCourse.id, title: "Functions in C", order: 3, language: "c", xpReward: 25, codeExample: `#include <stdio.h>\n\n// Function prototype (declaration before main)\nint add(int a, int b);\nvoid printLine(char ch, int count);\nint factorial(int n);\n\nint main() {\n    printf("3 + 4 = %d\\n", add(3, 4));\n    \n    printLine('=', 20);\n    printf("Factorial of 6 = %d\\n", factorial(6));\n    printLine('=', 20);\n    \n    return 0;\n}\n\n// Function definitions\nint add(int a, int b) {\n    return a + b;\n}\n\nvoid printLine(char ch, int count) {\n    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {\n        printf("%c", ch);\n    }\n    printf("\\n");\n}\n\nint factorial(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return 1;\n    return n * factorial(n - 1);\n}`, content: `Functions in C are the key to organized, reusable code. C requires you to declare functions before using them.\n\nFunction structure:\nreturnType functionName(parameter list) {\n    // body\n    return value;\n}\n\nFunction prototypes:\n• Declare function signature before main\n• Lets you define the function after main\n• Good practice: put prototypes at top of file\n• int add(int a, int b); — prototype\n\nReturn types:\n• void — no return value\n• int, double, char, etc. — single value\n• Pointers — return address of data\n\nParameter passing:\n• By VALUE — function gets a copy\n• Changes inside function don't affect original\n• To modify original, pass a POINTER\n\nScope:\n• Local variables — only exist in the function\n• Global variables — accessible everywhere (use sparingly)\n• static local — persists between calls\n\nRecursion:\n• Function calling itself\n• Must have base case (return without recursion)\n• Stack overflow risk for deep recursion\n• Elegant for: factorials, Fibonacci, tree traversal\n\nCall stack:\n• Each function call creates a stack frame\n• Contains: return address, parameters, local variables\n• Stack grows downward in memory` },
    { courseId: cCourse.id, title: "Pointers", order: 4, language: "c", xpReward: 35, codeExample: `#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    int x = 42;\n    int *ptr = &x;  // ptr stores address of x\n    \n    printf("x = %d\\n", x);         // 42\n    printf("&x = %p\\n", &x);       // address of x\n    printf("ptr = %p\\n", ptr);     // same address\n    printf("*ptr = %d\\n", *ptr);   // dereference: 42\n    \n    // Modify through pointer\n    *ptr = 100;\n    printf("x is now: %d\\n", x);  // 100\n    \n    // Pointer to pointer\n    int **pp = &ptr;\n    printf("**pp = %d\\n", **pp);  // 100\n    \n    // Pass by reference (modify original)\n    void doubleValue(int *n);\n    // ...\n    \n    // NULL pointer\n    int *null_ptr = NULL;\n    if (null_ptr == NULL) {\n        printf("Pointer is null\\n");\n    }\n    \n    // Array and pointers\n    int arr[] = {10, 20, 30, 40};\n    int *p = arr;  // points to first element\n    \n    printf("%d\\n", *p);      // 10\n    printf("%d\\n", *(p+1));  // 20 (pointer arithmetic)\n    printf("%d\\n", p[2]);   // 30 (same as *(p+2))\n    \n    return 0;\n}`, content: `Pointers are C's most powerful and most feared feature. They give you direct control over memory.\n\nWhat is a pointer?\nA pointer is a variable that stores a memory address.\nint *ptr — ptr holds the address of an int\n\nOperators:\n• & (address-of) — gets the address of a variable\n• * (dereference) — follows the pointer to get the value\n\nKey concepts:\n• int x = 10; — x stores the value 10\n• int *p = &x; — p stores the address of x\n• *p = 20; — changes x through the pointer\n\nCommon uses:\n1. Pass by reference — modify function arguments\n2. Dynamic memory allocation (malloc)\n3. Arrays — arrays ARE pointers in C\n4. Strings — char* is a pointer to first character\n5. Function pointers — pass functions as arguments\n\nPointer arithmetic:\n• p + 1 moves forward by sizeof(*p) bytes\n• Allows iterating arrays with pointers\n• p[i] is exactly equivalent to *(p+i)\n\nNULL pointer:\n• Special value 0 — doesn't point to anything\n• Always check for NULL before dereferencing!\n• Dereferencing NULL = segmentation fault (crash)\n\nDangerous mistakes:\n• Uninitialized pointer — garbage value\n• Dangling pointer — points to freed memory\n• Buffer overflow — writing past array bounds\n• Memory leak — forgetting to free` },
    { courseId: cCourse.id, title: "Memory Management", order: 5, language: "c", xpReward: 35, codeExample: `#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>  // malloc, calloc, realloc, free\n#include <string.h>  // memset, memcpy\n\nint main() {\n    // malloc - allocate memory (uninitialized)\n    int *arr = (int*)malloc(5 * sizeof(int));\n    if (arr == NULL) {\n        printf("Memory allocation failed!\\n");\n        return 1;\n    }\n    \n    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n        arr[i] = i * 10;\n    }\n    printf("arr[3] = %d\\n", arr[3]); // 30\n    \n    // realloc - resize\n    arr = (int*)realloc(arr, 10 * sizeof(int));\n    for (int i = 5; i < 10; i++) arr[i] = i * 10;\n    \n    // calloc - allocate AND zero-initialize\n    double *scores = (double*)calloc(5, sizeof(double));\n    printf("scores[0] = %.1f\\n", scores[0]); // 0.0 (zeroed)\n    \n    // Dynamic string\n    char *str = (char*)malloc(50 * sizeof(char));\n    strcpy(str, "Hello, Dynamic Memory!");\n    printf("%s\\n", str);\n    \n    // MUST free when done!\n    free(arr);\n    free(scores);\n    free(str);\n    \n    // Set to NULL after free\n    arr = NULL;\n    scores = NULL;\n    str = NULL;\n    \n    printf("Memory freed successfully\\n");\n    return 0;\n}`, content: `Manual memory management is C's defining characteristic. You control exactly when memory is allocated and freed.\n\nStack vs Heap:\n• Stack: automatic, function-scoped, fast, limited size\n  int x = 5; — on stack, freed when function returns\n• Heap: manual, unlimited (within RAM), persistent\n  malloc() — on heap, you decide when to free\n\nHeap allocation functions (from <stdlib.h>):\n• malloc(bytes) — allocate raw bytes (uninitialized)\n• calloc(n, size) — allocate n items of size, zero-initialized\n• realloc(ptr, new_size) — resize allocation\n• free(ptr) — release memory back to OS\n\nUsage pattern:\n1. Allocate: int *p = malloc(n * sizeof(int));\n2. Check for NULL! malloc can fail\n3. Use the memory: p[i] = value;\n4. Free when done: free(p);\n5. Set to NULL: p = NULL;\n\nMemory errors (serious bugs!):\n• Memory leak — forgot to free (program grows forever)\n• Double free — freeing already-freed memory (crash)\n• Use after free — accessing freed memory (undefined)\n• Buffer overflow — writing past allocated region (security!)\n• Null dereference — using NULL pointer (crash)\n\nTools for catching memory bugs:\n• Valgrind — memory error detector\n• AddressSanitizer — compile with -fsanitize=address\n• Static analyzers — clang-tidy` },
  ]);

